.TH SCYTALE 1 "2026-09-27" "scytale 0.8" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
scytale \- cryptographic command line tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scytale
.I command
.RI [ subcommand ]
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file ]
.br
.B scytale list
.RI [ family ]
.br
.B scytale
.I command
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B scytale
runs the operations of the scytale library from the command line, one
command per module of the library:
.BR hash ,
.BR mac ,
.BR aead ,
.BR cipher ,
.BR kdf ,
.BR key ,
.BR sig ,
.BR kex ,
.BR kem ,
.B pke
and
.BR random .
.PP
Input is standard input, or the
.I file
named last on the command line. Output is standard output, or the file
named by
.BR \-o .
A file that holds a secret, whether a generated key, a shared secret
or a derived key, is created readable by its owner alone.
.PP
Every option that carries bytes, such as a key, IV, nonce, tag, salt,
signature or label, is written with a prefix that says how to read it
(see
.BR VALUES ).
The algorithm fixes the length of a key, IV or nonce, and a value of
another length is refused: nothing is padded or cut to fit.
.SH VALUES
.TP
.BI hex: digits
Hex, in either case: no
.BR 0x ,
no separators, and an even count of digits.
.TP
.BI file: path
The raw bytes of the file, all of them.
.TP
.BI fd: n
The raw bytes read to the end of descriptor
.IR n ,
for
.B 3<
.I keyfile
in a script.
.TP
.BI env: name
The value of the environment variable
.IR name ,
as hex.
.TP
.BI str: text
The text itself. Allowed for additional data, labels, salts, contexts
and passwords; refused for a key, since a key typed as text is a
password without a key derivation.
.PP
A value without a prefix is an error. A key given as
.B hex:
on the command line is visible to every process on the machine through
.BR ps (1)
and stays in the shell's history, so
.B file:
or
.B fd:
is the better form for one; there is nothing wrong with
.B hex:
for a nonce or a salt, or in a script that already holds the key that
way.
.SH OPTIONS
Every option has a long form, and the long form is what the text
below refers to. The short forms, where a command has them:
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-algorithm
The algorithm, by the names
.B scytale list
prints.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key
A key value, or for the public-key commands a private key file.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-public
A public key file; for
.B kex agree
the option is
.BR \-\-peer .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-signature
A signature value.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-nonce
A nonce value.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-hash
A hash, by name.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-length
An output length in bytes.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-iterations
An iteration count.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-out
The output file, in place of standard output.
.TP
.BR \-\-in
The input key file, for
.B key public
and
.BR "key show" ,
in place of standard input.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
The help for a command, ending with the value syntax and the exit
codes.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
The version.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS list
.B scytale list
.RI [ family ]
.PP
Prints the algorithm names each command takes, one per line, for a
script to check. With a
.I family
(one of
.BR cipher ,
.BR aead ,
.BR hash ,
.BR xof ,
.BR mac ,
.BR key )
the names alone; without one, every name prefixed by its family.
.SS random
.B scytale random
.RI [ \-\-binary ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.I count
.PP
.I count
bytes from the system-seeded CTR_DRBG, as hex with a newline, or raw
with
.BR \-\-binary .
.SS hash
.B scytale hash
.RI [ "\-a algorithm" ]
.RI [ "\-l length" ]
.RI [ "\-\-function\-name value" ]
.RI [ "\-\-customization value" ]
.RI [ \-\-binary ]
.RI [ file... ]
.PP
A digest of each file, or of standard input, as hex followed by two
spaces and the file's name, in the layout of
.BR sha256sum (1);
standard input is named
.BR \- .
With
.B \-\-binary
the raw digest alone.
.TP
.BI \-a,\ \-\-algorithm\  name
The hash;
.B sha256
by default. One of
.BR sha1 ,
.BR sha224 ,
.BR sha256 ,
.BR sha384 ,
.BR sha512 ,
.BR sha512\-224 ,
.BR sha512\-256 ,
.BR sha3\-224 ,
.BR sha3\-256 ,
.BR sha3\-384 ,
.BR sha3\-512 ,
or an extendable-output function,
.BR shake128 ,
.BR shake256 ,
.B cshake128
or
.BR cshake256 .
.TP
.BI \-l,\ \-\-length\  bytes
The output length, required for an extendable-output function and
refused otherwise.
.TP
.BI \-\-function\-name\  value ,\ \-\-customization\  value
The cSHAKE function name and customization string, as
.BR VALUES ;
empty without them.
.SS mac
.B scytale mac
.BI \-a\  algorithm
.BI \-k\  key
.RI [ "\-\-verify tag" ]
.RI [ "\-l length" ]
.RI [ "\-\-customization value" ]
.RI [ \-\-binary ]
.RI [ file ]
.PP
A tag over the input, as hex, or raw with
.BR \-\-binary .
With
.B \-\-verify
the input is checked against
.I tag
instead: nothing is printed, and the exit status says whether it
matched.
.TP
.BI \-a,\ \-\-algorithm\  name
One of
.BI hmac\- hash
for any hash above
.RB ( hmac\-sha256 ,
.BR hmac\-sha3\-512 ,
\&...),
.BR cmac\-aes\-128 ,
.BR cmac\-aes\-192 ,
.BR cmac\-aes\-256 ,
.BR kmac128 ,
.B kmac256
or
.BR poly1305 .
.TP
.BI \-k,\ \-\-key\  value
The key. HMAC and KMAC take any length; CMAC takes the cipher's, and
Poly1305 32 bytes. A Poly1305 key is for one message only.
.TP
.BI \-l,\ \-\-length\  bytes
The tag length, for KMAC: 32 bytes for
.B kmac128
and 64 for
.B kmac256
without it.
.TP
.BI \-\-customization\  value
The KMAC customization string.
.SS aead encrypt, aead decrypt
.B scytale aead
.RB { encrypt | decrypt }
.BI \-a\  algorithm
.BI \-k\  key
.BI \-n\  nonce
.RI [ "\-\-aad value" ]
.RI [ "\-\-tag\-length bytes" ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.RI [ file ]
.PP
Authenticated encryption. Encryption writes the ciphertext and then the
tag; decryption reads ciphertext and tag as one input, checks the tag,
and writes the plaintext only if it checked. A message that fails
leaves standard output empty and exits with status 1. Encryption with
GCM and ChaCha20-Poly1305 streams; decryption reads the whole input
first, since plaintext handed out before the tag is checked is
plaintext an attacker chose.
.TP
.BI \-a,\ \-\-algorithm\  name
One of
.BR aes\-128\-gcm ,
.BR aes\-192\-gcm ,
.BR aes\-256\-gcm ,
.BR aes\-128\-gcm\-siv ,
.BR aes\-256\-gcm\-siv ,
.BR aes\-128\-ccm ,
.BR aes\-192\-ccm ,
.BR aes\-256\-ccm ,
.BR aes\-128\-xpn ,
.BR aes\-192\-xpn ,
.BR aes\-256\-xpn ,
.BR chacha20\-poly1305 .
.TP
.BI \-k,\ \-\-key\  value
The key, of the width the name says: 16, 24 or 32 bytes for AES, 32
for ChaCha20-Poly1305.
.TP
.BI \-n,\ \-\-nonce\  value
The nonce. Twelve bytes, except that GCM takes any length, CCM takes 7
to 13, and XPN takes 24: the 12-byte salt followed by the 12-byte
frame. A nonce must never repeat under one key.
.TP
.BI \-\-aad\  value
Additional data, authenticated but not encrypted; the same value must
be given to decrypt. Empty without it.
.TP
.BI \-\-tag\-length\  bytes
The tag length, 16 by default. GCM and XPN take 4 to 16, CCM an even
number from 4 to 16; GCM-SIV and ChaCha20-Poly1305 take 16 only.
.SS cipher encrypt, cipher decrypt
.B scytale cipher
.RB { encrypt | decrypt }
.BI \-a\  algorithm
.BI \-k\  key
.RI [ "\-\-iv value" ]
.RI [ "\-n nonce" ]
.RI [ "\-\-counter n" ]
.RI [ "\-\-padding pkcs7|none" ]
.RI [ "\-\-alphabet chars" ]
.RI [ "\-\-tweak value" ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.RI [ file ]
.PP
The unauthenticated modes. Nothing here checks that a message is what
was encrypted; a script that needs to know that wants
.BR aead .
These are for a protocol that brings its own MAC, a disk, a key to
wrap, or a format to preserve.
.TP
.BI \-a,\ \-\-algorithm\  name
.BI aes\- width \- mode
for a
.I width
of 128, 192 or 256 and a
.I mode
of
.BR ecb ,
.BR cbc ,
.BR ctr ,
.BR cfb1 ,
.BR cfb8 ,
.BR cfb128 ,
.BR ofb ,
.BR xts ,
.B kw
or
.BR kwp ;
.BR chacha20 ;
.BI ff1\-aes\- width
or
.BI ff3\-1\-aes\- width
for the format-preserving modes.
.TP
.BI \-k,\ \-\-key\  value
The key, of the cipher's width. XTS takes two keys of that width, one
after the other, as
.BR openssl (1)
does, and they must differ.
.TP
.BI \-\-iv\  value
One block: the IV for CBC, CFB and OFB, the initial counter block for
CTR, the tweak for XTS. Required by those modes and refused by ECB, KW
and KWP.
.TP
.BI \-n,\ \-\-nonce\  value ,\ \-\-counter\  n
The 12-byte nonce and the initial block counter (0 without it) for
ChaCha20, which encrypts and decrypts with the one operation.
.TP
.BI \-\-padding\  pkcs7 | none
For ECB, CBC and CFB128, which take whole blocks. PKCS#7 padding is
added on encryption and removed on decryption unless
.B none
is given, in which case an input that is not whole blocks is refused.
PKCS#7 is what
.B openssl enc
and CMS use. Padding that does not check on decryption exits with
status 1 and prints nothing; a script must not report that
difference to anyone who can submit ciphertexts.
.TP
.BI \-\-alphabet\  chars ,\ \-\-tweak\  value
For FF1 and FF3-1, which read and write lines of text in the alphabet,
one message per line. The alphabet is the string of characters that
are the numerals, in order; the decimal digits without it. The tweak
is any length for FF1 and exactly 7 bytes for FF3-1; empty for FF1
without it. These modes are not constant time.
.PP
KW wraps a key of any whole number of 8-byte blocks, at least two; KWP
wraps any length. Both add 8 bytes. CTR, OFB and CFB take any length
and stream; CBC, CFB128 and ECB stream a block at a time; CFB1, XTS,
KW and KWP read the whole input.
.SS kdf hkdf
.B scytale kdf hkdf
.RI [ "\-H hash" ]
.BI \-\-ikm\  value
.RI [ "\-\-salt value" ]
.RI [ "\-\-info value" ...]
.BI \-l\  bytes
.RI [ \-\-binary ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.PP
HKDF (RFC 5869): keys from keying material that is already
unguessable, such as a shared secret. The input keying material may
not be given as
.BR str: .
.B \-\-info
may repeat; the values are concatenated in order. Output as hex, or raw
with
.BR \-\-binary .
.SS kdf pbkdf2
.B scytale kdf pbkdf2
.RI [ "\-H hash" ]
.BI \-\-password\  value
.BI \-\-salt\  value
.BI \-i\  iterations
.BI \-l\  bytes
.RI [ \-\-binary ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.PP
PBKDF2 (RFC 8018): a key from a password, which is guessable, at a
cost per guess set by the iteration count. The password and salt may
be given as
.BR str: .
.SS key generate
.B scytale key generate
.BI \-a\  algorithm
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.PP
A new private key, as a PEM
.B PRIVATE KEY
block (PKCS#8), to standard output or to
.IR file ,
created readable by its owner alone. The algorithm is one of
.BR ed25519 ,
.BR x25519 ,
.BR ecdsa\-p256 ,
.BR ecdsa\-p384 ,
.BR ecdh\-p256 ,
.BR ecdh\-p384 ,
.BI rsa\- bits
for any
.I bits
from 1024 to 8192,
.BR ml\-kem\-512 ,
.BR ml\-kem\-768 ,
.BR ml\-kem\-1024 ,
.BR ml\-dsa\-44 ,
.BR ml\-dsa\-65 ,
.BR ml\-dsa\-87 ,
or
.BI slh\-dsa\- hash \- size s
and
.BI slh\-dsa\- hash \- size f
for a
.I hash
of
.B sha2
or
.B shake
and a
.I size
of 128, 192 or 256. Keys on P-256 and P-384 serve both ECDSA and ECDH;
an RSA key serves both signatures and encryption.
.SS key public
.B scytale key public
.RI [ "\-\-in file" ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.PP
The public half of a private key, as a PEM
.B PUBLIC KEY
block.
.SS key show
.B scytale key show
.RI [ "\-\-in file" ]
.PP
Prints what a key file holds: its algorithm, which half it is, and
for RSA the modulus length.
.PP
Every command that takes a key file reads PEM: the
.B PRIVATE KEY
and
.B PUBLIC KEY
blocks that
.BR openssl (1)
and everything else write, and the bare
.BR "RSA PRIVATE KEY" ,
.B RSA PUBLIC KEY
and
.B EC PRIVATE KEY
forms as well. The algorithm is read out of the file, so it is never
named on the command line.
.SS sig sign
.B scytale sig sign
.BI \-k\  private
.RI [ "\-H hash" ]
.RI [ "\-\-context value" ]
.RI [ "\-\-rsa\-scheme pss|pkcs1" ]
.RI [ \-\-raw ]
.RI [ \-\-hex ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.RI [ file ]
.PP
Signs the input with the private key in
.IR private ,
writing the signature raw, or as hex with
.BR \-\-hex .
.SS sig verify
.B scytale sig verify
.BI \-p\  public
.BI \-s\  signature
.RI [ "\-H hash" ]
.RI [ "\-\-context value" ]
.RI [ "\-\-rsa\-scheme pss|pkcs1" ]
.RI [ \-\-raw ]
.RI [ file ]
.PP
Checks
.I signature
(a value, as
.BR VALUES )
over the input under the public key in
.IR public .
Nothing is printed; the exit status is 0 if it verified and 1 if not.
A private key file is refused here;
.B key public
makes the public one.
.TP
.BI \-H,\ \-\-hash\  name
The hash for ECDSA and RSA,
.B sha256
by default; Ed25519, ML-DSA and SLH-DSA hash internally and ignore it.
.TP
.BI \-\-context\  value
The context string for Ed25519 (which then signs in the Ed25519ctx
form), ML-DSA and SLH-DSA; up to 255 bytes. Empty without it, which
for Ed25519 is the plain form.
.TP
.BI \-\-rsa\-scheme\  pss | pkcs1
RSASSA-PSS, salted with a hash's worth of random bytes, or the
deterministic RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5;
.B pss
by default.
.TP
.B \-\-raw
An ECDSA signature as the fixed-width
.I r
followed by
.IR s ,
rather than the DER SEQUENCE that
.B openssl dgst
writes. Ed25519, ML-DSA and SLH-DSA signatures are always the
fixed-width bytes their standards define, and RSA signatures the
modulus length.
.SS kex agree
.B scytale kex agree
.BI \-k\  private
.BI \-p\  peer
.RI [ \-\-hex ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.PP
The shared secret between the private key in
.I private
and the peer's public key in
.IR peer ,
which must be on the same curve: X25519, P-256 or P-384. Raw bytes,
or hex with
.BR \-\-hex .
A shared secret is keying material, not a key; derive keys from it with
.BR "kdf hkdf" .
.SS kem encapsulate
.B scytale kem encapsulate
.BI \-p\  public
.BI \-\-secret\-out\  file
.RI [ \-\-hex ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.PP
A ciphertext for the ML-KEM public key in
.IR public ,
to standard output or
.BR \-o ,
and the shared secret it carries to
.IR file ,
created readable by its owner alone. The peer recovers the secret from
the ciphertext with the private key.
.SS kem decapsulate
.B scytale kem decapsulate
.BI \-k\  private
.RI [ \-\-hex ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.RI [ file ]
.PP
The shared secret an ML-KEM ciphertext carries. A ciphertext that was
not made for the key yields a secret that matches nothing, rather than
an error, as FIPS 203 requires.
.SS pke encrypt, pke decrypt
.B scytale pke
.RB { encrypt | decrypt }
.RB { "\-p public" | "\-k private" }
.RI [ "\-H hash" ]
.RI [ "\-\-label value" ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.RI [ file ]
.PP
RSA-OAEP: a short message, at most the modulus length less twice the
hash length less two bytes, under an RSA public key. The hash is
.B sha256
by default, the label empty; both must match to decrypt. A ciphertext
that does not decrypt exits with status 1 and says nothing more.
.SH ALGORITHM NAMES
.B scytale list
prints every name. Names are lower case with hyphens, and name the
whole construction, key width included:
.BR aes\-256\-gcm ,
.BR hmac\-sha3\-256 ,
.BR ml\-dsa\-65 .
A key of another width than the name says is refused rather than
taken as a hint.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a verification that passed.
.TP
.B 1
A tag, signature or padding did not verify: the message is not what
was made under this key.
.TP
.B 2
The request could not be carried out as asked: an unknown name, an
option the algorithm does not take, a value in the wrong form, a key
of the wrong length, a file that is not a key.
.TP
.B 3
Anything else: a file that would not open, a generator that would not
seed.
.PP
Errors go to standard error as
.B scytale:
followed by the message, and never contain key material.
.SH EXAMPLES
Encrypt a file to a key held in a file, with a header authenticated
alongside it, and decrypt it again:
.PP
.nf
scytale random 32 \-\-binary \-o session.key
scytale random 12 > nonce.hex
scytale aead encrypt \-a aes\-256\-gcm \-k file:session.key \e
    \-n hex:$(cat nonce.hex) \-\-aad str:v1 report.pdf \-o report.sealed
scytale aead decrypt \-a aes\-256\-gcm \-k file:session.key \e
    \-n hex:$(cat nonce.hex) \-\-aad str:v1 report.sealed \-o report.pdf
.fi
.PP
Sign a release with a post-quantum key and check the signature:
.PP
.nf
scytale key generate \-a ml\-dsa\-65 \-o release.pem
scytale key public \-\-in release.pem \-o release.pub
scytale sig sign \-k release.pem release.tar \-o release.sig
scytale sig verify \-p release.pub \-s file:release.sig release.tar
.fi
.PP
Agree a key with a peer and derive session keys from it:
.PP
.nf
scytale key generate \-a x25519 \-o me.pem
scytale key public \-\-in me.pem \-o me.pub
scytale kex agree \-k me.pem \-p peer.pub \-o shared.bin
scytale kdf hkdf \-\-ikm file:shared.bin \-\-salt str:session\-1 \e
    \-\-info str:encrypt \-\-length 32
.fi
.PP
Read a key from a descriptor rather than the command line:
.PP
.nf
scytale mac \-a hmac\-sha256 \-k fd:3 message.txt 3< mac.key
.fi
.PP
Decrypt what
.B openssl enc
encrypted:
.PP
.nf
openssl enc \-aes\-128\-cbc \-K $KEY \-iv $IV < plain > cipher
scytale cipher decrypt \-a aes\-128\-cbc \-k hex:$KEY \-\-iv hex:$IV < cipher
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR openssl (1),
.BR sha256sum (1).
.PP
The library's documentation, at https://docs.rs/scytale, describes
each algorithm, its limits and its security properties; the tool adds
nothing to them.
.SH AUTHOR
Michael Paddon.
